<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daniel Defoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1660–<a href="/years/1731">1731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daniel Defoe was an English writer, journalist and merchant. A Dissenter and pamphleteer, he was pilloried for satire in 1703 and later worked as a government agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> during the negotiations for the Union of 1707. His novels Robinson Crusoe (1719) and Moll <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> (1722), written in a realistic first-person style, made him one of the founders of the English novel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daniel-defoe.jpg" alt="Daniel Defoe" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UnknownUnknown , style of Sir Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
